WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:20.700
Hi, I'm Kevin Alaka, I'm the trends manager at YouTube and I professionally watch YouTube

00:20.700 --> 00:21.700
videos.

00:21.700 --> 00:22.700
It's true.

00:22.700 --> 00:26.920
So we're going to talk a little bit today about how videos go viral and then why that

00:26.920 --> 00:28.560
even matters.

00:28.560 --> 00:33.200
We all want to be stars, celebrities, singers, comedians.

00:33.200 --> 00:36.580
And when I was younger, that seemed so very, very hard to do.

00:36.580 --> 00:40.120
But now web video has made it so that any of us or any of the creative things that we

00:40.120 --> 00:43.480
do can become completely famous and a part of our world's culture.

00:43.480 --> 00:47.800
I mean, any one of you could be famous on the internet by next Saturday.

00:47.800 --> 00:52.340
But there are over 48 hours of video uploaded to YouTube every minute.

00:52.340 --> 00:56.400
And of that, only a tiny percentage ever goes viral and gets tons of views and becomes a

00:56.400 --> 00:57.440
cultural moment.

00:57.440 --> 00:59.820
So how does it happen?

00:59.820 --> 01:00.820
Three things.

01:00.820 --> 01:04.800
Tastemakers, communities of participation, and unexpectedness.

01:04.800 --> 01:07.160
All right, let's go.

01:07.160 --> 01:20.960
Oh my God, oh my God, oh my God, oh my God, oh my God, oh my God, oh wow.

01:20.960 --> 01:24.920
Last year, Bear Vasquez posted this video that he had shot outside his home in Yosemite

01:24.920 --> 01:25.920
National Park.

01:25.920 --> 01:30.840
In 2010, it was viewed 23 million times.

01:30.840 --> 01:35.760
This is a chart of what it looked like when it first became popular last summer.

01:35.760 --> 01:38.760
But he didn't actually set out to make a viral video, Bear.

01:38.760 --> 01:42.320
He just wanted to share a rainbow because that's what you do when your name is Yosemite

01:42.320 --> 01:45.660
Mountain Bear.

01:45.660 --> 01:49.960
And he had posted lots of nature videos, in fact, and this video had actually been posted

01:49.960 --> 01:52.500
all the way back in January.

01:52.500 --> 01:55.040
So what happened here?

01:55.040 --> 01:57.200
Jimmy Kimmel, actually.

01:57.200 --> 02:01.300
Jimmy Kimmel posted this tweet that would eventually propel the video to be as popular

02:01.300 --> 02:06.320
as it had become because tastemakers like Jimmy Kimmel introduced us to new and interesting

02:06.320 --> 02:21.800
things and bring them to a larger audience.

02:21.800 --> 02:25.160
So you didn't think that we could actually have this conversation without talking about

02:25.160 --> 02:27.280
this video, I hope.

02:27.280 --> 02:30.080
Rebecca Black's Friday is one of the most popular videos of the year.

02:30.080 --> 02:34.080
It's been seen nearly 200 million times this year.

02:34.080 --> 02:37.360
This is a chart of what it looked like, and similar to Double Rainbow, it seems to have

02:37.360 --> 02:40.780
just sprouted up out of nowhere.

02:40.780 --> 02:42.720
So what happened on this day?

02:42.720 --> 02:45.920
Well, it was a Friday, this is true.

02:45.920 --> 02:54.160
And if you're wondering about those other spikes, those are also Fridays.

02:54.160 --> 02:55.540
But what about this day?

02:55.540 --> 02:57.360
This one particular Friday?

02:57.360 --> 03:01.000
Well, Tosh.no picked it up, a lot of blogs started writing about it.

03:01.000 --> 03:04.740
Michael J. Nelson from Mystery Science Theater was one of the first people to post a joke

03:04.740 --> 03:06.560
about the video on Twitter.

03:06.560 --> 03:10.020
But what's important is that an individual or a group of tastemakers took a point of

03:10.020 --> 03:14.480
view and they shared that with a larger audience, accelerating the process.

03:14.560 --> 03:18.440
So then this community formed of people who shared this big inside joke and they started

03:18.440 --> 03:23.360
talking about it and doing things with it, and now there are 10,000 parodies of Friday

03:23.360 --> 03:24.360
on YouTube.

03:24.360 --> 03:33.720
Even in the first seven days, there was one parody for every other day of the week.

03:33.720 --> 03:38.160
Unlike the one-way entertainment of the 20th century, this community participation is how

03:38.160 --> 03:42.520
we become a part of the phenomenon, either by spreading it or doing something new with it.

03:44.480 --> 03:57.040
So, Nyan Cat is a looped animation with loop music.

03:57.040 --> 03:59.000
It's this, just like this.

03:59.000 --> 04:03.240
It's been viewed nearly 50 million times this year.

04:03.240 --> 04:07.080
And if you think that that is weird, you should know that there is a three-hour version of

04:07.080 --> 04:12.040
this that's been viewed four million times.

04:12.040 --> 04:18.880
Some cats were watching this video.

04:18.880 --> 04:30.560
Cats were watching other cats watch this video.

04:30.560 --> 04:37.080
But what's important here is the creativity that it inspired amongst this techie, geeky

04:37.080 --> 04:38.280
internet culture.

04:38.280 --> 04:42.560
There were remixes.

04:42.560 --> 05:04.520
Someone made an old-timey version, and then it went international.

05:04.520 --> 05:10.000
An entire remix community sprouted up that brought it from being just a stupid joke to

05:10.000 --> 05:12.680
something that we could all actually be a part of.

05:12.680 --> 05:19.400
Because we don't just enjoy now, we participate.

05:19.400 --> 05:20.960
And who could have predicted any of this?

05:20.960 --> 05:24.800
Who could have predicted Double Rainbow or Rebecca Black or Nyan Cat?

05:24.800 --> 05:30.340
What script could you have written that would have contained this in it?

05:30.340 --> 05:34.700
In a world where over two days of video get uploaded every minute, only that which is

05:34.700 --> 05:38.660
truly unique and unexpected can stand out in the way that these things have.

05:38.660 --> 05:43.220
When a friend of mine told me that I needed to see this great video about a guy protesting

05:43.220 --> 06:08.820
bicycle fines in New York City, I admit I wasn't very interested.

06:08.820 --> 06:14.700
By being totally surprising and humorous, Casey Neistat got his funny idea and point

06:14.700 --> 06:17.420
seen five million times.

06:17.420 --> 06:21.260
And so this approach holds for anything new that we do creatively.

06:21.260 --> 06:33.660
And so it all brings us to one big question.

06:33.660 --> 06:36.180
What does it mean?

06:36.180 --> 06:41.620
Music makers, creative participating communities, complete unexpectedness, these are characteristics

06:41.620 --> 06:47.140
of a new kind of media and a new kind of culture where anyone has access and the audience defines

06:47.140 --> 06:48.140
the popularity.

06:48.140 --> 06:52.540
I mean, as mentioned earlier, one of the biggest stars in the world right now, Justin Bieber,

06:52.540 --> 06:54.400
got his start on YouTube.

06:54.400 --> 06:56.980
No one has to green light your idea.

06:56.980 --> 07:00.680
And we all now feel some ownership in our own pop culture.

07:00.680 --> 07:05.720
And these are not characteristics of old media, and they're barely true of the media of today.

07:05.760 --> 07:08.640
But they will define the entertainment of the future.

07:08.640 --> 07:09.000
Thank you.

